near_miss_filing:
w3b-v5 EM-7 search — Bash grep (full lines), `sed -n` on every long hit, all four corpora, by SITE as well as by invariant id, with an observed positive control. This row's filing status had never been established: the pre-migration ladder could not say anything about filing outside two arms. THE FILING IS A SITE DOCBLOCK, WHICH IS WHY FOUR CORPUS SEARCHES BY ID WOULD HAVE MISSED IT. packages/rph-application/src/handlers/pwa-authoring.ts:993-996, inside `publishPwa`'s `advanceStatus` options, verbatim:

    // In-arrow: VALIDATED only. A re-publish of an already-PUBLISHED PWA rewrote rootPwuTypeId — editing a version
    // this file's own header and editPwa both declare IMMUTABLE, and bypassing the single-root composition gate,
    // which runs on ValidatePwa and not here. Undertakings bind pwaVersion, which does not change, so they would
    // silently inherit a different root.

and at :1007-1010, immediately above `eventPayload: () => ({})`:

    // REG-F-020 ledger: `PwaPublishedPayloadSchema` is an EMPTY strictObject — publication is a fact about the
    // aggregate the envelope already identifies, and the event carries no fields at all. The command payload's
    // optional `rootPwuTypeId` was being passed through and rejected. It is not lost: `mutate` above writes it
    // onto the PWA's state, which is where a reader looks for it.

Under REG-D-039's precedent a site docblock is a legitimate filing, and these two record BOTH code facts the row's `owed` names — the re-root write and the composition-gate bypass, and the empty event payload — in the file the remedy would touch.

WHY IT DOES NOT COVER THE ROW'S OWED. What the first docblock MEASURES is the RE-PUBLISH arrangement (PUBLISHED -> PUBLISHED), and what it CLOSES is that arrangement: the remedy it justifies is `precondition: fromStates('VALIDATED')` on the line below (:998), and the refuter's own drive confirms it fires — "B1. PublishPwa {rootPwuTypeId: GOVERNED} (re-root AGAIN) -> REJECTED | RPH_ILLEGAL_STATE_TRANSITION | PublishPwa requires ... to be VALIDATED, but it is PUBLISHED." The arrangement the row owes is the FIRST publish, on the VALIDATED -> PUBLISHED arrow, which that precondition admits by design: "A. PublishPwa {rootPwuTypeId: BARE} (the unpoliced subtree) -> ACCEPTED", `publicationStatus = PUBLISHED | rootPwuTypeId = pwut_...R43 (validated root was pwut_...R41)`, `PwaPublished events = 1 | payloads = [{}]`. `mutate` at :1005 (`return p.rootPwuTypeId ? { ...base, rootPwuTypeId: p.rootPwuTypeId } : base;`) accepts a caller-supplied root the composition gate never saw, and the second docblock frames the empty payload as correct-by-ledger rather than as a hole. The docblock names the hazard; the remedy it applied closes only half of it.

SECOND NEAR-MISS, in the corpus the searches keep forgetting: docs/_working/HARMONIZATION-LOG.md:1874-1877 names the identical code fact — "Their `payloadFields: []` emits `z.strictObject({})`, which means **\"nobody specified this\", not \"this payload is empty\"**. Enforcing it would have rejected `PublishPwa` for the *extra* key `rootPwuTypeId` — forcing a handler to **strip a real field, recorded in object state, to satisfy the absence of a spec**. That is the inverse of the defect the gate exists for." WHY IT IS NOT A FILING OF THIS LIMB: what it MEASURES is the ratified-event-gate MEMBERSHIP RULE and what it CLOSES is that rule ("ratified citation AND non-empty `payloadFields`"); it records the field's absence from the event as a REASON TO EXCLUDE the five PWA events from enforcement, and calls the opposite "the inverse of the defect the gate exists for". A filer working from it would harden the gate and leave the publication write untouched.

⚠ RE-SEARCHED INDEPENDENTLY 2026-08-23 (blind adjudication, w3b-v6), by a reader who could not see the text above: THREE near-misses, all of which a later reader will land on, none of which closes this limb.

1. THE DOCBLOCK AT THE ADJACENT SITE — the strongest decoy, because it is at the same file and names this invariant's own canon line. packages/rph-application/src/handlers/pwa-authoring.ts:898-906, verbatim: "NOT enforced here, deliberately — the assurance-assignment limb of L1639: see `pwa-validation-gate.test.ts`'s skipped case. §11.7.4 makes the floor \"🔒 de minimis floor · non-removable\" on EVERY PWU Type (so it is never an assignment that can go *missing*) … L1639's \"A missing policy assignment blocks PWA validation/publication\" is therefore relative to APPLICABILITY … Un-skip when the declaration is contracted (§16 item 9) and route through `evaluateApplicability`." That is a genuine §16 deferral of ASR-19 LIMB 1 — the "missing required policy assignment cannot become validated or published" clause, at ValidatePwa. It says nothing about a publication or activation step LOWERING an assurance requirement that was assigned; a MISSING assignment and a DOWNGRADED one are different arrangements at different transitions. Anyone grepping ASR-19 or L1639 will find this docblock and stop.

2. REG-005:740 (REG-F-020) — records `PwaPublished` as one of six events GIVEN the declared shape through kit.ts's `eventPayload` seam. It CERTIFIES the empty payload as conforming. It is the opposite of a filing against `eventPayload: () => ({})`, and that empty payload is precisely what makes the re-root silent.

3. docs/_working/HARMONIZATION-LOG.md:1874 (and the same reasoning copied into packages/rph-contracts/src/gen/gen-messages.ts:280) — "Enforcing it would have rejected `PublishPwa` for the *extra* key `rootPwuTypeId` — forcing a handler to **strip a real field, recorded in object state, to satisfy the absence of a spec**." This defends KEEPING the unvalidated `rootPwuTypeId` on the publish command; it treats the field as legitimate and never asks whether the publication step may rewrite it. docs/_working/AUDIT-shape-survivorship-2026-08-20.md:17 is a field-list fidelity row scored ENFORCED and is silent on the publish write. REG-005:8275/:8293 are REG-F-220's Undertaking field census (they merely list `instantiationProfile` among persisted fields); docs/_working/HARMONIZATION-FINDINGS.md:104 is struck through (~~…~~), i.e. refuted, and was about the free-string profile enum, not a downgrade.

REG-005:10813 and :6165-6200 are floor/version-binding entries (REG-F-202 and the ASR-3 limb-5/6 EditPwa under-bump finding) — they concern an UNMET or STALE floor, which the row itself and the refuter both assign to ASR-3/ASR-12, not to this limb.

