WHAT SURVIVED. I re-ran lane-ASR-6-a.mjs and every quoted line reproduces byte-for-byte, including 'L3a ProposeEvidence limitations OMITTED -> VALIDATION_FAILED | RPH_VALIDATION_SCHEMA_FAILED', 'L3b InvalidateEvidence while PROPOSED -> REJECTED | RPH_ILLEGAL_STATE_TRANSITION | InvalidateEvidence requires EVIDENCE evd_...V01 to be ADMISSIBLE, but it is PROPOSED...', the ACCEPTED control on the same axis, and 'L3b RE-AdmitEvidence on the INVALIDATED object -> REJECTED | RPH_ILLEGAL_STATE_TRANSITION | AdmitEvidence requires EVIDENCE evd_...V02 to be PROPOSED, but it is INVALIDATED...'. The census reproduces exactly: `grep -rn "limitations" --include=*.test.ts packages/rph-application packages/rph-contracts verif | grep -ci "delete\|omit\|without\|missing"` -> 0 against a same-population control of 18 FILES; dwp03 hits at :182 and :203; the three-file run is 'Test Files 3 passed (3) / Tests 27 passed (27)'. R4: the anchor resolves once, at 537. So the LIMB IS ENFORCED — the arm, not the finding, is what I am overturning.

DEFECT 1 — CONJUNCT B IS HELD BY TWO INDEPENDENT LIVE MECHANISMS, WHICH I PROVED BY DRIVING THE DOMAIN KERNEL DIRECTLY (scratchpad/ref-ASR6-matrix3.mjs):
  import { canTransition, classifyTransition } from '@janumipwb/rph-domain';
  Evidence.status INVALIDATED -> ADMISSIBLE : canTransition=false | classify={"klass":"ILLEGAL_UNDEFINED","reason":"transition INVALIDATED -> ADMISSIBLE is not in the Evidence.status matrix"}
  Evidence.status PROPOSED   -> ADMISSIBLE : canTransition=true  | classify={"klass":"LEGAL"}
and from ref-ASR6-matrix2.mjs, STATE_MACHINES['Evidence.status'].transitions = 'PROPOSED->ADMISSIBLE | PROPOSED->REJECTED | ADMISSIBLE->INVALIDATED | ADMISSIBLE->SUPERSEDED', IN-ARROWS to ADMISSIBLE = PROPOSED, terminalStates = [REJECTED, SUPERSEDED, INVALIDATED]. advanceStatus runs precondition (kit.ts:763-779) THEN guard (kit.ts:781) THEN `checkTransition` (kit.ts:803). So the DWP-03 precondition wins the race — I confirmed the observed message text comes from command-precondition.ts:134, not from the matrix — but the matrix would refuse the same arrangement on its own. This DIRECTLY REFUTES the row's own 'HONEST NARROWING': it says a widening to `fromStates('PROPOSED','INVALIDATED')` 'would leave :182 green, so the specific INVALIDATED arm I drove is not separately pinned'. It would leave :182 green AND STILL BE REFUSED, by checkTransition with ILLEGAL_UNDEFINED. The closure is stronger than the row claims and it is not single-mechanism, which is precisely the premise the row used to rule MULTI_SITE out.

DEFECT 2 — CONJUNCT A'S SITE IS NAMED IN NO STRUCTURED FIELD, AND THE FIELD THAT IS NAMED IS A COMMENT. `enforcing_site` = assurance.ts:537 with anchor 'JAN-CMDPRE DWP-03: single in-arrow to ADMISSIBLE, from PROPOSED (Evidence.status)'. I read 535-542: 537-538 are the COMMENT; the code is `precondition: fromStates('PROPOSED'),` at **539**. A comment refuses nothing. (The lane likely chose it for R4 uniqueness — `grep -c -F "precondition: fromStates('PROPOSED')" assurance.ts` -> 2 — i.e. it traded the enforcing line for a unique one.) Conjunct A's actual sites appear only in prose: messages.ts:193 (`limitations: z.array(z.string())`, required on a strictObject ProposeEvidencePayloadSchema) and objects.ts:516 (required on EvidenceObjectSchema, re-validated on every commit by kit.ts commitState `validateAgainst(entry.schema, args.nextState, ...)`, which is why `limitations` can never be dropped by any later write either). Two conjuncts, three live mechanisms, four sites, two packages.

